# Line endings are decided by the repository rather than by each machine's core.autocrlf. Every
# text file is stored LF and checked out in the platform's own convention; without this the rule
# holds only as long as everyone's git happens to be configured the same way, and one clone with
# core.autocrlf=false starts committing CRLF blobs that diff as whole rewritten files.
#
# `text=auto` and not `*.kt eol=lf`: two Kotlin sources embed real NUL bytes (the EXIF identifier
# is written as a literal Exif followed by two NUL bytes), so git detects them as binary. auto
# leaves them alone; forcing text on them would rewrite them for no benefit.
#
# First, not last: within this file a later rule wins, so the two `-text` pins below rely on this
# blanket rule sitting above them. Moved beneath, it would quietly re-enable conversion on the
# dictionary the pin exists to protect.
* text=auto

# The PP-OCRv5 weights are ~22MB of binary that would otherwise sit in every clone forever. LFS
# keeps them out of the packfile — at the cost of needing git-lfs installed to get real files
# rather than pointers. `ppocrv5_dict.txt` is deliberately not tracked here: it is 74KB of plain
# text, so it diffs and reviews normally.
feature/ocr/src/main/assets/ocr/ppocrv5/*.onnx filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# The dictionary maps the recogniser's output indices back to characters, one per line, and its
# digest is documented beside it. Left out of line-ending conversion so a checkout on Windows is
# byte-identical to what was published — otherwise the file on disk no longer matches the SHA-256
# it is documented by, and every entry carries a trailing carriage return.
feature/ocr/src/main/assets/ocr/ppocrv5/ppocrv5_dict.txt -text
